From e2d5b30badae7387bfed11761408766bbd41fe1b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: autoresearch <agent@localhost>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 06:51:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] ggml: count activation elements quantised by mul_mat,
 behind GGML_COUNT_QUANT

Every mul_mat converts its F32 src1 to the vec_dot type - Q8_0 for every quantised weight type - and a
transformer block feeds the SAME activation tensor to three or four matmuls (q/k/v, the FFN's two projections,
attention output), so the same elements are converted repeatedly. The kernel brief lists memoising that
conversion as the first thing to try, because it is bit-identical and needs no new kernel, but whether it is
worth building depends entirely on the volume - and guessing is exactly the mistake of starting an f16
converter before checking that f16 was even the faster path.

So measure it. Counted once per call (ith == 0) because the quantisation loop is sliced across threads by ne10
blocks: counting per thread would multiply the volume by the thread count and make the number a fiction. The
counter is off unless GGML_COUNT_QUANT is set, and even then it is one relaxed atomic per matmul.
---
 src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c b/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
index 7bcd349..722f049 100644
--- a/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -1350,6 +1350,11 @@ UseGgmlGemm1:;
             }
         }
     #else
+        // Counted once per call (ith == 0): the loop below is sliced across threads by ne10 blocks, so
+        // counting per thread would multiply the volume by the thread count and make it a fiction.
+        if (ggml_count_quant_enabled() && ith == 0) {
+            g_ggml_quant_elems += (uint64_t) ne10 * (uint64_t) ne11 * (uint64_t) ne12 * (uint64_t) ne13;
+        }
         for (int64_t i13 = 0; i13 < ne13; ++i13) {
             for (int64_t i12 = 0; i12 < ne12; ++i12) {
                 for (int64_t i11 = 0; i11 < ne11; ++i11) {
